            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Follow the thread of a conversation in role-plays.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details in spoken weather announcements (e.g., 'There will be rain tomorrow.').<br>
            Can recognize project-related spoken instructions for activities (e.g., 'Bring your materials tomorrow.').<br>
            Can follow the thread of conversations in role-plays about the weather (e.g., 'What's the weather like today?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows students large flashcards or pictures of different weather conditions (e.g., a bright sun, heavy rain, strong wind, fluffy clouds). The teacher plays short audio clips of weather sounds (e.g., rain falling, wind blowing). Students listen and point to the matching picture. The teacher asks, "What do you hear?" and "What is the weather like?" to activate prior knowledge related to weather.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words related to weather (e.g., <i>sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, temperature, forecast, will be, should bring</i>). The teacher clearly pronounces each word and models intonation for weather predictions, emphasizing the last word (e.g., "It will be <b>sunny</b>."). The teacher uses gestures and realia (e.g., a small umbrella) to clarify meaning. For 'thermostat', the teacher shows a picture and models segmenting 'ther-mo-stat' into syllables for phonemic awareness practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with questions like:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>forecast</i> about today or tomorrow?" (Expected answer: Tomorrow)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If it's <i>rainy</i>, should you bring a hat or an umbrella?" (Expected answer: An umbrella)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, authentic-like audio recording of a weather announcement/dialogue (e.g., a radio weather report). The audio script will include the target vocabulary (<i>weather, forecast, report, rain, sun, wind, temperature, prediction, sunshine, cloudy, sunny, rainy, windy, will be, predict, look like, bring</i>) and future simple tense.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Audio Transcript: "Good morning! Here is your weather <b>forecast</b> for tomorrow. It <b>will be cloudy</b> in the <b>morning</b> with some <b>wind</b> in the east. In the <b>afternoon</b>, we <b>predict rain</b>. The <b>temperature will be</b> around 25 degrees Celsius. It <b>looks like rain</b> tomorrow, so you <b>should bring</b> an umbrella!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listen for gist and specific details in spoken weather announcements. Recognize future simple tense for predictions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. "What is this audio about?" (Expected answer: The weather / the weather for tomorrow).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and decide if the following statements are True or False:
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Tomorrow morning will be sunny." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It will rain in the afternoon tomorrow." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should bring an umbrella." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks or pictures. Students listen and match the predicted weather condition to the time of day (morning/afternoon).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific vocabulary (<i>sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, temperature, forecast</i>), future simple tense ('It will be...'), prepositions of place ('in the east').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Draw/Match):</b> The teacher says a weather prediction using target vocabulary (e.g., "Tomorrow, it will be sunny." "The wind is in the north."). Students listen and draw a simple weather symbol on their mini-whiteboards or match it to a picture on their worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with cloze sentences related to weather predictions. The teacher plays short audio sentences, and students listen and fill in the missing weather words, 'will be', or prepositions.
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "Tomorrow, it ________ (will be) ________ (cloudy)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "The ________ (rain) is ________ (in) the west."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Repeat/Check):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays short, simple weather prediction sentences (e.g., "It will be rainy tomorrow in the evening. You should bring an umbrella."). Student A listens and repeats the sentence to Student B. Student B listens to Student A and confirms if it sounds correct based on what they heard from the teacher. They practice the intonation, emphasizing the last word.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> "Our Mini Weather Report Role-Play".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in pairs to create a short role-play (3-4 turns each) about tomorrow's weather. One student acts as a "Weather Reporter" and the other as an "Interviewer" or "Listener." They will practice asking about the forecast and describing weather conditions, focusing on listening carefully to their partner's predictions and responding appropriately. This task directly addresses the specific objective: "Follow the thread of a conversation in role-plays."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher assigns each pair a specific region (e.g., "Chiriquí," "Panama City") for their forecast.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students decide who is the "Reporter" and who is the "Interviewer."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. The "Interviewer" initiates the conversation, asking about the forecast (e.g., "Hello, what's the weather forecast for tomorrow in Chiriquí?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The "Reporter" provides a simple prediction using future simple, target vocabulary, and prepositions of place (e.g., "Tomorrow, it will be sunny in the morning, but expect rain in the afternoon over the mountains. The temperature will be warm.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. The "Interviewer" listens and asks a follow-up question or makes a comment, showing they understood (e.g., "So, rain in the afternoon? I should bring my umbrella then?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">6. The "Reporter" responds (e.g., "Yes, you should bring an umbrella.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, monitoring for correct use of grammar, vocabulary, and active listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Our Mini Weather Report Role-Play." The teacher will note students' ability to understand and respond appropriately in the conversation, demonstrating their listening comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their short weather report role-play for another pair or the class. The teacher assesses their listening and speaking interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening Focus):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Follows Conversation:</b> Can the student respond appropriately to their partner's questions/statements? (e.g., asking relevant follow-up questions, confirming details).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifies Details:</b> Does the student demonstrate understanding of specific weather conditions and times mentioned by their partner?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Uses Target Vocabulary:</b> Does the student understand and use new vocabulary (e.g., <i>rainy, sunny, will be, forecast</i>) correctly?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Is the student's speech clear enough for their partner to understand? (Sociolinguistic: Engage in peer discussions on weather-related topics).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving class.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I understood most of my partner's weather report." (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List 2 weather words you heard today."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the weather report was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult, and what can I do next time to listen better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the students demonstrate adequate listening comprehension during the audio tasks and role-plays? What specific instances showed this?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the audio materials (e.g., speed, clarity, vocabulary density) appropriate for A2.1 learners? What adjustments, if any, are needed for future listening activities?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific scaffolds (e.g., more visual aids, pre-listening questions, explicit instruction on intonation) proved most effective for listening, and which need reinforcement for the next lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we listened to learn about weather. In our next lesson (Lesson 2), we will read a short weather report and a simple text to see how these weather words look in writing and how they help us understand a weather forecast. We will use the same vocabulary we learned today."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>